\section{UML Diagram}

\subsection{General Introduction}

The Object Management Group (OMG) specification states:\\

The Unified Modelling Language (UML) is a graphical language for visualizing, specifying, constructing, and documenting the artefacts of a software-intensive system. The UML offers a standard way to write a system’s blueprints, including conceptual things such as business processes and system functions as well as concrete things such as programming language statements, database schemas, and reusable software components.
\begin{itemize}
\item The unified modelling language allows the software engineer to express an analysis model using the modelling notation that is governed by a set of syntactic semantic and pragmatic rules.
\item A UML system is represented using five different views that describe the system from distinctly different perspective. UML is specifically constructed through two different domains they are
\item UML Analysis modelling, this focuses on the user model and structural model views of the system.
\item UML design modelling, which focuses on the behavioural modelling, implementation modelling and environmental model views.
\end{itemize}

\begin{flushleft}
  These are divided into the following types.
\end{flushleft}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Use case diagram
\item Class diagram
\item Sequence diagram
\item Collaboration diagram
\item Activity diagram
\item State chart diagram
\end{enumerate}


\newpage
\subsection{Use Case Diagram}
Use case diagrams are usually referred to as behavior diagrams used to describe a set of actions (use cases) that some system or systems (subject) should or can perform in collaboration with one or more external users of the system (actors). Each use case should provide some observable and valuable result to the actors or other stake holders of the system.\\


 The usage scenario describes a real-world example of how the users interact with a system. It describes the steps, events, and/or actions which occur during the interaction. Usage scenarios are applied in several development processes. The use case diagram of proposed system is described in above figures.\\

 A use case is a description of a set of sequences of  actions, including variants, that a system performs to yield an observable result of value to an actor. Graphically, a use case is rendered as an ellipse. A use case describes what a system (or a subsystem, class, or interface) does but it does not specify how it does it.\\

\begin{flushleft}
  The primary goals of Use Case diagrams include:
\end{flushleft}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[-]	Providing a high-level view of what the system does.
\item[-]	Identifying the users ("actors") of the system.
\item[-]	Determining areas needing human-computer interfaces.
\end{enumerate}

\newpage

\begin{flushleft}
  \textbf{Graphical Notation:}
\end{flushleft}


 The basic components of Use Case diagrams are the Actor, the Use Case and the Association.


