\section{Summary}
This gives a detail explanation of the architecture. A data flow diagram (DFD) gives a graphical representation of the “flow” of data through an information system, modeling its process aspects. It gives the overview of the system which is later elaborated. Each level gives a detail explanation about each module used at a specific level. A class diagram is a type of static structure diagram that describes the structure of a system by showing the system’s classes, their attributes, operations (or methods), and the relationships among the classes. Use case diagram gives description of a set of sequence of actions, including variants etc. that a system performs to yield an observable result of value to an actor. A single diagram is not enough to cover all aspects of the system. So UML defines various kinds of diagrams to cover most of the aspects of a system. You can also create your own set of diagrams to meet your requirements. Diagrams are generally made in an incremental and iterative way.


\chapter{IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS}

Source code of AODV routing protocol is modified in NS-2 (v2.30) to implement the proposed routing protocol. Note that the proposed NCPR protocol needs Hello packets to obtain the neighbor information, and also needs to carry the neighbor list in the RREQ packet. Therefore, in the implementation, some techniques are used to reduce the overhead of Hello packets.\\

The approach is for probabilistic rebroadcasting by taking effectively advantage of the neighbor coverage knowledge, so rebroadcast delay and rebroadcast probability is calculated.\\




\begin{flushleft}
  \textbf{Rebroadcast delay:}
\end{flushleft}

The rebroadcast delay is to determine the forwarding order. The node    which has more
common neighbors with the previous node has the lower delay, if this node rebroadcasts a packet, then more common neighbors will know this fact. Therefore, this rebroadcast delay enables the information that the nodes have transmitted the packet spread to more neighbors, which is the key to success for the implemented scheme.\\


\begin{flushleft}
  \textbf{Rebroadcast probability:}
\end{flushleft}

 The scheme considers the information about the uncovered neighbors (UCN), connectivity metric and local node density to calculate the rebroadcast probability.

\begin{flushleft}
  The rebroadcast probability is composed of two parts:
\end{flushleft}


\begin{itemize}

\item Additional Coverage Ratio: Which is the ratio of the number of nodes that should be covered by a single broadcast to the total number of neighbors;

\item Connectivity Factor: This reflects the relationship of network connectivity and the number of neighbors of a given node.

\end{itemize}

The approach combines the compensation of the neighbor coverage knowledge and the probabilistic mechanism, which appreciably decrease the number of retransmissions so reducing the routing overhead, and with improved the routing performance.

\newpage

\begin{flushleft}
  The approach can be described as:
\end{flushleft}

\begin{enumerate}

\item When node receives an RREQ packet from its previous node, it can use the neighbor list in the RREQ packet to estimate how many its neighbors have not been covered by the RREQ packet from its previous node.

\item If node has more neighbors uncovered by the RREQ packet from previous node, which means that if node rebroadcasts the RREQ packet, the RREQ packet can reach more additional neighbor nodes.

\item When a neighbor receives an RREQ packet, it could calculate the rebroadcast delay according to the neighbor list in the RREQ packet and its own neighbor list.

\item After determining the rebroadcast delay, the node can set its own timer.

\item If node receives a duplicate RREQ packet from its neighbor node, it knows that how many its neighbors have been covered by the RREQ packet from neighbor node. Thus, node could further adjust its UCN set according to the neighbor list in the RREQ packet from neighbor node.

\item After adjusting the Uncovered Neighbors set, the RREQ packet received from node is discarded.

\item When the timer of the rebroadcast delay of node expires, the node obtains the final UCN set. The nodes belonging to the final UCN set are the nodes that need to receive and process the RREQ packet.

\item Calculate the additional coverage ratio which is the ratio of the number of nodes that are additionally covered by this rebroadcast to the total number of neighbors of node.

\item Calculate connectivity factor which is the ratio of the number of nodes that need to receive the RREQ packet to the total number of neighbors of node.

\item Combining the additional coverage ratio and connectivity factor, the rebroadcast probability of node can be obtained to forward the packets.

\end{enumerate}

\newpage

The figure \ref{fig15} shows the sample flowchart of work for optimizing the broadcasting by taking the advantage neighbor coverage technique.\\

\begin{figure}[h]
\begin{center}
\scalebox{0.7}{\includegraphics{fc.jpg}}
\caption{Flow Chart for NCPR Routing Protocol Algorithm}\label{fig15}
\end{center}
\end{figure}



\section{Algorithm Steps}


\subsection{Network Formation}

\begin{enumerate}
\item The network contains number of nodes.
\item In this module, can construct a topology to provide communication paths for wireless network.
\item Here the node will give the own details  such as Node ID and port number through which the transmission is done and similarly give the known nodes details such as Node ID, IP address and port number which are neighbors to given node.

\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Rebroadcast Delay determination}

\begin{enumerate}

\item This module is to calculate the rebroadcast by using a novel scheme .
\item The rebroadcast delay is to determine the forwarding order.
\item The node which has the more common neighbors with the previous one has the lower delay.
\item In order to reduce the collisions in the network for each node maintains a delay time.

\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Rebroadcast Probability Determination}

In this module novel scheme is use to calculate rebroadcast probability. The scheme considers the information about the uncovered neighbors. The Rebroadcast Probability composed of  two parts ,they  are

\begin{enumerate}
\item Additional coverage ratio
\item Connectivity factor
\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Neighbor Coverage-Based Probabilistic Rebroadcast}

\begin{enumerate}

\item The proposed NCPR protocol needs Hello packets to obtain the neighbor information and also needs to carry the neighbor list in the RREQ packet.\\
\item Therefore, some techniques are used to reduce the overhead of Hello packets and neighbor list in the RREQ packet.\\
\item Since a node sending any broadcasting packets can inform its neighbors of its existence, the broadcasting packets such as RREQ and route error (RERR) can play a role of Hello packets. only when the time elapsed from the last broadcasting packet is greater than the value of HelloInterval, the node needs to send a Hello packet.\\
\item In order to reduce the overhead of neighbor list in the RREQ packet, each node needs to monitor the variation of its neighbor table and maintain a cache of the neighbor list in the received RREQ packet.\\
\end{enumerate}


\newpage

\section{Algorithm Description}
Below algorithm 1 is showing neighbor coverage based probabilistic rebroadcast approach for reducing the network routing overhead.

\subsection{Algorithm 1}


\begin{flushleft}
 Definitions:
\textbf{RREQv:} RREQ packet received from node v.\\
\textbf{Rv.id:} the unique identifier (id) of RREQv.\\
\textbf{N (u):} Neighbour set of node u.\\
\textbf{U (u, x):} Uncovered neighbours set of node u for RREQ whose id is x.\\
\textbf{Timer (u, x):} Timer of node u for RREQ packet whose id isx.\\
\end{flushleft}

{Note that, in the actual implementation of NCPR protocol, every different RREQ needs a
    UCN set and a Timer.}




 \begin{enumerate}

\item if ni receives a new RREQs from s then
\item {Compute initial uncovered neighbours set U(ni,Rs.id) forRREQs:}
\item U(ni,Rs.id) = N(ni) − [N(ni) ∩ N(s)] − {s}
\item {Compute the rebroadcast delay Td(ni):}
\item Tp(ni) = 1 − [|N(s)∩ N(ni)| / |N(s)|]
\item Td (ni) = MaxDelay × Tp(ni)
\item Set a Timer (ni,Rs.id) according to Td(ni)
\item end if
\item
\item while ni receives a duplicate RREQj from nj beforeTimer (ni,Rs.id) expires do
\item {Adjust U(ni,Rs.id):}
\item U(ni,Rs.id)=(ni,Rs.id)−[U(ni,Rs.id)∩N(nj )]
\item discard (RREQj );
\item end while
\item
\item if Timer (ni,Rs.id) expires then
\item {Compute the rebroadcast probability Pre(ni):}
\item Ra (ni) = |U(ni,Rs.id)|/ |N(ni)|
\item Fc (ni) = Nc/ |N(ni)|
\item Pre (ni) = Fc(ni) • Ra(ni)
\item if Random (0,1) ≤ Pre(ni) then
\item broadcast (RREQs)
\item else
\item discard (RREQs)
\item end if

\end{enumerate}

\section{Simulation Environment}
	
In order to evaluate the performance of the proposed NCPR protocol, compare it with some other protocols using the NS-2 simulator. Broadcasting is a fundamental and effective data dissemination mechanism for many applications in MANETs. In this project, only one of the applications is considered: route request in route discovery. In order to compare the routing performance of the proposed NCPR protocol have to compare with other routing protocol i.e. AODV protocol which is an optimization scheme for reducing the overhead of RREQ packet incurred in route discovery in the recent literature.\\
	
	Simulation parameters are as follows: The Distributed Coordination Function (DCF) of the IEEE 802.11 protocol is used as the MAC layer protocol. The radio channel model follows a Lucent’s WaveLAN with a bit rate of 2 Mbps, and the transmission range is 250 meters. We consider constant bit rate (CBR) data traffic and randomly choose different source-destination connections. Every source sends four CBR packets whose size is 512 bytes per second. The mobility model is based on the random waypoint model in a field of 1000m X 1000m. In this mobility model, each node moves to a random selected destination with a random speed from a uniform distribution [1, max-speed].\\

	After the node reaches its destination, it stops for a pause-time interval and chooses a new destination and speed. In order to reflect the network mobility, we set the max-speed to 5 m/s and set the pause time to 0. The MaxDelay used to determine the rebroadcast delay is set to 0.01 s, which is equal to the upper limit of the random jitter time of sending broadcast packets in the default implementation of AODV in NS-2. Thus, it could not induce extra delay in the route discovery. The simulation time for each simulation scenario is set to 100 seconds.

